  "AI content payment is not merely a copyright or transaction-efficiency problem; it is a question of political-economic legitimacy. As AI valuations reach hundreds of billions, society asks why the gains from public web content and human knowledge accrue to a few firms. The paper proposes a six-layer tiered data market — from free public content through scarce verified datasets to public benefit-sharing — and defines the AI network democratic economy: rights, pricing, participation, auditability, and returns, each democratized through machine-readable protocols like AICR/AICL.": "Yoɓugol loowdi AI wonaa tan caɗeele jeytaare winndannde walla yaawre coggu; ko tippudi hulɓinaare politik-ekonomi. So tawii teeŋtinal AI heɓtii ɗiggol keemtaali hujunere doola, renndo ina naamnoo ko saabii ɓural loowdi rewrew feeñundi e anndal aade ngal hersa e juuɗe deenaali seeɗa. Winndannde ndee ina waɗana luumo daawal jeegom sowe — iwde e loowdi feeñundi mo yoɓaaka, haa e daawal goongɗinaandi famɗundi, haa naftoore renndo — ɗum firtoo ekonomi rewrew AI demokaraasiwo: jojjanɗi, teeŋtingol coggu, jokkondiral, goongɗingol, e barkeeji, kala majji ina demokaraasinee tawi ko e porotokoolji ko masin nanata wano AICR/AICL.",
